# Supabase -> Project Settings -> Data API / API Keys
# Both are safe to expose to the browser: the anon key is protected by the RLS
# policies in supabase/schema.sql. Never put the service_role key in here.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# --- AI extraction pipeline (scripts/extract) -------------------------------
# Used ONLY by `npm run extract`, which runs on your machine. Never imported by
# anything under src/, and never given a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix — the service-role
# key bypasses every RLS policy in the schema.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key

# Preferred for volume: the free tier is request-bound with a generous token
# allowance, and 1M context removes the passage-budget squeeze entirely.
# Key: https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=
# Optional. Default gemini-2.0-flash. `npm run extract -- --models` lists yours.
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.0-flash
# Optional. Free-tier requests per minute for your model.
GEMINI_RPM=15

GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_...
# Optional. Default llama-3.3-70b-versatile. `npm run extract -- --models` lists
# what your key can reach; openai/gpt-oss-* models accept json_schema natively,
# which saves ~800 tokens per call versus the json_object fallback.
GROQ_MODEL=llama-3.3-70b-versatile
# Optional. Tokens per minute for your tier and model — Groq console → Limits.
# The pipeline sizes every request to 85% of this, so getting it right matters
# more than it looks: too high and you get 429s, too low and you waste context.
GROQ_TPM=12000

# --- Crawler manners (scripts/extract/page.ts) ------------------------------
# All optional. The defaults are what the pipeline was tuned on; these exist for
# when a particular site needs gentler treatment, or when you want to identify
# yourself differently. A site's own `Crawl-delay` always wins over these, and a
# site that publishes one is dropped to a single connection.

# Minimum gap between request STARTS to one host, in milliseconds.
CRAWL_DELAY_MS=800
# Connections open to one host at once. 1 makes the crawl strictly serial.
CRAWL_HOST_CONCURRENCY=2
# How we announce ourselves. Keep a contact URL in it.
CRAWL_USER_AGENT=ScholarTrackBot/1.0 (scholarship deadline verification; +https://github.com/)
# Used only after a host answers 401/403/406/451 to the agent above — a WAF
# rejecting the shape of the request rather than the URL. robots.txt is obeyed
# before either identity is used.
CRAWL_FALLBACK_USER_AGENT=

# --- Scheduled refresh (src/app/api/cron/refresh/route.ts) ------------------
# The catalogue re-reading itself once a week, so a deadline that has passed
# gets a fresh look instead of sitting on the dashboard until someone notices.
# It STAGES proposals like any other run — nothing is applied without a human.
#
# Required, or the endpoint refuses every request: Vercel Cron sends this as
# `Authorization: Bearer <value>`. Generate one with `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# The schedule itself lives in vercel.json.
CRON_SECRET=
# The account the refresh runs AS. An ordinary Supabase user that you have added
# to the `admins` table — not the service-role key, which must never reach the
# deployed app (see docs/deployment.md). RLS applies to every write it makes, and
# revoking it is deleting one allowlist row rather than rotating a master key.
CRON_SUPABASE_EMAIL=
CRON_SUPABASE_PASSWORD=
# Optional. Rows re-read per scheduled run. Deliberately small: a steady trickle
# keeps the catalogue honest without a weekly stampede at every funder's site,
# and a free-tier model quota is the other ceiling.
CRON_REFRESH_LIMIT=12

# --- Reaching pages a plain fetch cannot read (both optional) ---------------
# Two categories of page yielded NOTHING before these existed, and a page that
# yields nothing is a programme with every field blank — not one field. Both are
# a URL rather than a dependency on purpose: bundling a headless browser or an
# OCR toolchain would put a native install into a project that installs clean
# today and deploys to a serverless host.
#
# Neither is a first resort. `scripts/extract/hydrate.ts` runs a ladder that is
# free before it reaches either of these — most "JavaScript-rendered" pages ship
# their content as a JSON hydration payload inside the HTML we already
# downloaded, and are recovered with no extra request at all.

# A headless renderer, for pages whose content really is built client-side.
# Contract: POST {"url": "..."} -> {"html": "..."} (or the HTML as the body).
# Anything satisfying that works — browserless, a small Playwright worker.
# RENDER_SERVICE_URL=https://renderer.example.com/render
# RENDER_SERVICE_TOKEN=
# RENDER_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# An OCR engine, for the scanned PDFs some funders publish their rules in.
# Contract: POST the raw PDF bytes -> {"text": "..."} (or the text as the body).
#
# READ THIS BEFORE ENABLING IT. OCR output is a machine's reading of an image,
# so grounding a quote against it is a weaker claim than grounding against text
# somebody published. That is why it is not a loosening of grounding but a
# fourth evidence tier: quotes matched on an OCR'd page are recorded as
# `match: "ocr"`, the review UI flags them, and a human is told which readings
# rest on a guess at a glyph. Leave it unset and scans are reported as
# unreadable, exactly as before.
# OCR_SERVICE_URL=https://ocr.example.com/read
# OCR_SERVICE_TOKEN=
# OCR_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
